# The facility.
#
# A neoclassical public building in the Ionic order: a hexastyle portico facing south on a
# podium reached by a broad flight of steps, a main block with a rusticated ground storey and a
# piano nobile above it, wings east and west, and a domed rotunda on the centre line.
#
# This file is a manifest and almost nothing else. The building is written in the fragments it
# includes, one to a file, because a building is made of parts and so should the thing that
# describes it be — and because that way many hands can work on it at once without any two of
# them editing the same lines.
#
# The order matters. `_contract.clip` first, because it holds every dimension the rest derives
# from and every material they are made of. `_order.clip` second, because it holds the Ionic
# vocabulary — a column, a pilaster, a capital, a baluster — that the parts place and repeat.
# Then the parts, outside to inside, and the paint stacks in the order they are included: a
# later coat covers an earlier one, so the base coat is here at the top and every fragment paints
# only where its own shapes are.
#
# See clips/facility/BRIEF.md for what each part is and the rules they are all written to.

include "facility/_contract.clip"
include "facility/_order.clip"

# The coat everything starts in. Limestone is what the building is, and every other material in
# it is an exception argued for by one of the fragments below.
paint limestone

# --- the ground it stands on ---------------------------------------------------------------
include "facility/site.clip"
include "facility/podium.clip"
include "facility/steps.clip"

# --- the shell -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
include "facility/walls.clip"
include "facility/pilasters.clip"
include "facility/windows.clip"
include "facility/doors.clip"
include "facility/portico.clip"
include "facility/entablature.clip"
include "facility/pediment.clip"
include "facility/parapet.clip"
include "facility/roof.clip"

# --- the dome ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
include "facility/drum.clip"
include "facility/dome.clip"

# --- what is inside it --------------------------------------------------------------------------
include "facility/vestibule.clip"
include "facility/rotunda.clip"
include "facility/halls.clip"
include "facility/stair.clip"

# --- and the state rooms, which are new -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Four corners of the main block had never been entered. The brief has always said the building
# has to be able to GROW -- "adding a room is one new file and three lines in the manifest" -- and
# these are those lines. They go here, after the four original rooms and before the fittings,
# because the fittings line below has to stay last among the inside parts for the reason its own
# comment gives.
#
# THE INTERIORS ARE NOT MIRRORED AND THE SHELL STILL IS. A salon east and a chapel west, a
# ballroom over one and a library over the other: mirroring them would mean two salons and no
# chapel. BRIEF.md rule 3 was amended in the same change to say the shell is symmetric and what
# is inside a room is not required to be.
include "facility/crypt.clip"
include "facility/salon.clip"
include "facility/chapel.clip"
include "facility/ballroom.clip"
include "facility/library.clip"
include "facility/enfilade.clip"
include "facility/terrace.clip"

include "facility/fittings.clip"

# --- and what the weather has done to all of it ------------------------------------------------
#
# Last, and that is the whole of why it is here rather than up with the site it mostly acts on.
# Weathering appends its coats after the author's, so whatever is included last is painted over
# everything before it — which is exactly right for sand, moss and stain, and exactly wrong if it
# is included in the middle.
#
# This line was missing for the building's whole first life. The fragment was written, the three
# `weather` statements in it were correct, and nothing included the file — so the one subsystem
# the facility exists to exercise was silently absent, and the only sign of it was that the report
# said one weathering coat where there should have been thirteen.
include "facility/surface.clip"

# --- assembly ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

let shell = union {
    part_site part_podium part_steps
    part_walls part_pilasters part_windows part_doors
    part_portico part_entablature part_pediment part_parapet part_roof
    part_drum part_dome
}

let inside = union {
    part_vestibule part_rotunda part_halls part_stair part_surface
    part_crypt part_salon part_chapel part_ballroom part_library part_enfilade
}

let built = union { shell inside }

# The voids come last, so nothing put in afterwards can be eaten by a room being carved. The
# fragments that need to remove matter — a doorway through a wall, a window through it, the
# rooms themselves — each expose one shape named `<part>_void`, and they are all taken away
# here, in one place, where the order is visible.
let hollowed = difference {
    built
    void_doors void_windows void_vestibule void_rotunda void_halls void_stair
    void_crypt void_salon void_chapel void_ballroom void_library void_enfilade
}

# AND THE FURNITURE AFTER THE ROOMS, which is what the paragraph above always claimed happened.
#
# It did not. `part_fittings` was in `inside`, `inside` is in `built`, and every room's void is
# subtracted from `built` — so every sconce, bench and statue standing in a room was cut away by
# the room it stands in. A room's void is correctly written as *the air of that room less that
# room's own stone*, which is what stops another fragment leaving a wall in somebody's room; but a
# fitting is BY DEFINITION a thing standing in somebody's air, and it went with it.
#
# What survived was the 0.09 m of backplate buried in the wall: a flat dark rectangle with no
# bracket, no bowl and no flame. Reported from playing as *"i cant tell what they are"*, which is
# the exact right reaction to the back of a lamp. The benches, buried the same 0.09 and projecting
# 0.45 into the room, went altogether, and so did both hall statues.
#
# It also took the light with it. halls.clip builds two rooms with no window in either and says in
# its own header that everything past the first bay arrives from a sconce or from nothing; the
# eight sconces are that "or from nothing", and with their bowls and flames cut off the halls were
# lit by nothing at all. The whole emissive load of the building indoors was one deleted union.
#
# fittings.clip diagnosed this from inside its own file, measured it, wrote the fix out in
# clips/facility/requests/fittings.md and could not make it, because the line is here. This is that
# line. It cannot open a seam: part_fittings removes no matter, exposes no void of its own, and
# overlaps its hosts by 0.05 everywhere. The grain still runs over it, because `all` displaces what
# comes out of here and not what went in.
# A DOOR AND A WINDOW STILL CUT A FITTING. A ROOM DOES NOT. That is the whole of the distinction
# this line exists to make, and the first version of it did not make it: `union { hollowed
# part_fittings }` put the furniture back after EVERY void, so a sconce standing in a doorway was
# no longer cut by the doorway. Reported from playing, immediately, as a lamp on one of the lesser
# doors -- a bronze bracket with its bowl alight, hanging in mid-air in front of a closed door
# leaf, which is what the portico's outer sconce has always been and what `void_doors` had always
# quietly been hiding.
#
# The two kinds of void are not the same kind of thing and it took a wrong picture to see it:
#
#   void_vestibule, void_rotunda, void_halls, void_stair   the AIR OF A ROOM. A fitting stands in
#       a room's air by definition, and subtracting these deleted every sconce, bench and statue
#       indoors (D608). They must not touch part_fittings.
#   void_doors, void_windows                               an OPENING THROUGH A WALL. Nothing may
#       stand in one -- not a fitting, not anything -- because the hole is the point. These must
#       still cut part_fittings, exactly as they did before D608.
#
# LEFT FOR fittings.clip AND NOT FIXED HERE: the outer portico sconce is at x = +-5.40 and the side
# door's void is x = 4.50 .. 6.30, so it is placed squarely in a doorway and comes out cut in half.
# That is where it was before D608 too; the change only made it visible for a day. The file's own
# note reasons the placement from "the great door's dressing stops at 2.025 and the antae start at
# 6.05" and never mentions the side doorcase, which runs 4.05 .. 6.75 straight through the middle
# of that span. Moving it is an elevation decision and belongs in the file that draws the elevation.
# AND THE ROOF GARDEN WITH THE FURNITURE, for the same reason and not quite the same one.
#
# `part_terrace` is pots, seats, lanterns, an armillary sphere and a cistern standing on the lead
# deck. Like a fitting it stands in somebody's air and must not be cut by a room; UNLIKE a fitting
# it is not in a doorway and never can be, because every door and window in this building is at
# least two and a half metres below the deck it stands on. So it joins here rather than in
# `inside`, and it does NOT go through the door-and-window difference: passing it through one
# would be a subtraction that can only ever remove something by accident.
let furnished = union { hollowed  difference { part_fittings void_doors void_windows }  part_terrace }

# --- surface --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Twelve millimetres of grain over everything — AND THE SAMPLER DROPS IT, AT EVERY RESOLUTION THIS
# GAME IS EVER BUILT AT. The line stays; the comment that used to sit here does not, because it was
# wrong and it was the load-bearing sentence in two other documents.
#
# It read: "under half a voxel at full detail, so it moves no wall — and is the difference between
# a face that reads as carved stone and one that reads as a drawing of carved stone." Both halves
# cannot be true at once, and `usable_displacement` in src/forge/clip_script.cpp quotes this exact
# line back and says why: being under a voxel is not what makes a displacement harmless, it is
# precisely what makes it DITHER, because a displacement too small to move a wall is still large
# enough to flip the voxels the wall is made of. On the flat axis-aligned faces this building is
# mostly made of, the whole face dithers at once — a raspy speckle that became black pepper once
# the renderer had real sun and sky, because a flipped voxel exposes a face pointing a different
# way and a differently-facing face is several times darker.
#
# So anything under HALF A VOXEL is dropped rather than scaled up, and 0.012 m is 0.38 of a voxel
# at metre 32 and 0.10 at metre 8. There is no resolution this building is sampled at where this
# line does anything at all, and the log has said so in two WARN lines on every single build:
#
#     displace amount 0.012 m is 0.38 of a voxel at metre 32 -- ... Dropped.
#     weather  amount 0.0045 m is 0.14 of a voxel at metre 32 -- ... Dropped.
#
# THE BEHAVIOUR IS CORRECT AND IS NOT BEING CHANGED HERE. Raising the amount to 0.016 would make it
# apply and would bring back the pepper; the honest answer at this grid is the undisplaced surface,
# which is what the sampler already gives. What was wrong was a comment claiming a visual effect
# the build has not produced for as long as the number has read 0.012 — and BRIEF.md rule 5, whose
# whole justification for `below=0.02` was this grain pushing voxels outside their own shape. It
# does not. The rind that rule prevents is real, but it comes from coverage-versus-centre on
# transformed geometry, which is why that rule now names 0.035 as well.
let all = displace { furnished grain_fine } amount=0.012


variation colour=0.030 rough=0.070 seed=17

solid all

# --- where the origin is ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The player spawns at 0,0,0 and should be standing in the middle of the rotunda floor looking out
# of the main door, so the BUILDING moves to meet that rather than the spawn moving to meet the
# building. The origin is the one coordinate everybody types; it should mean something.
#
# The rotunda floor is a cylinder centred at y 1.7325 and 0.135 thick, so its top face is at 1.80.
# The origin wants to be an eye height above it: 1.80 + 1.70 = 3.50 m down. Its centre is already at
# x 0, z 0 — the plan was drawn around it — so nothing moves sideways, and the main door is the
# opening on the minus-z face, which is the way the spawn looks.
#
# `origin` and not a translate around the solid. A translate moves the stone and leaves every paint
# rule at the height it was drawn for, which put the plinth course and the ground line partway up
# the walls — geometry right, colour wrong. This moves both.
origin 0 -3.50 0
